• Run this command to download the ubuntu image with the latest tag
docker image pull ubuntu:latest

• Run this command to list the images on the machine
docker image ls 


• Run this command to launch an instance of ubuntu and interact with it
docker container run -it ubuntu:latest    


• We can restart a stopped container by using the command docker start followed by the container name or id. For example, if the container is called practical_murdock (the names are randomly generated), we can run the following command:
docker container start practical_murdock



• Be careful to change the name of the container with yours !
docker container stop mystifying_matsumoto


• In a command, run the following command to run a container in the background
docker container run --detach --name my_ubuntu ubuntu:latest bash



• Stop and delete the container
docker container stop my_ubuntu
docker container rm my_ubuntu


• By adding the -rm or --remove argument, we can force the deletion of a container at shutdown.	Re-launch this container with this new argument
docker container run --rm --detach --name my_ubuntu ubuntu:latest bash

• One of the last simple arguments you can use to start a container is -e. This argument allows you to create an environment variable. This is like defining a variable with an export 	•  for example. This command can be very useful for passing specific arguments to a container.
	Run the following command to run a container with a my_variable environment variable
    
• docker container run -it --rm --name my_ubuntu -e "my_variable='hello everyone'" ubuntu:latest bash 	Once in the container, run the following command to check the contents of the variable my_variable
echo $my_variable  



Argument	            Use
--------------------  ---  
-it or --interactive	allows you to interact with the container via the command issued.
-d or --detach	      Allows you to launch the container in the background
-n or --name	        Allows you to choose the name of the container
--rm	                Force deletion of the container after it has stopped
-e	                  Allows you to define an environment variable in the container

	Start a ubuntu container named my_ubuntu in the background. We will not force the deletion of the container when it is stopped
docker container run -d --name my_ubuntu ubuntu:latest 



  	• You can use the command docker container inspect followed by the container id or name to be able to see the container properties:
	Run this command to inspect the container
docker container inspect my_ubuntu
  
